 "note": [
  "Nashville's two pools. `hers` is a song Taylor wrote and never released; `decoys` are real",
  "country songs by other people from the same few years. The page prints one title and asks",
  "which it is, so EVERY rule below exists to keep the answer in the player's memory of her",
  "rather than in the shape of the words.",
  "",
  "NOTHING HERE IS INVENTED. A made-up title can only be written in a register, and a register",
  "is a tell a player learns instead of learning the songs. Every decoy is a real track on the",
  "record named beside it.",
  "",
  "NO DECOY WAS A CHARTING SINGLE. Recognising a hit is general 2000s radio memory, not",
  "knowledge of her, and a recognised decoy is a free page. Singles were checked against each",
  "ARTIST'S DISCOGRAPHY rather than the album's page: Kellie Pickler's \"Didn't You Know How",
  "Much I Loved You\" is an album cut on Small Town Girl and was re-recorded as a #14 single in",
  "2009, which only the discography says. Album singles lists disagree with discographies more",
  "often than you would think — two of the fifteen did.",
  "",
  "NOTHING ON EITHER SIDE HAS A DOMINANT OWNER. A title that is also a famous song by somebody",
  "else breaks the page in the direction that matters most: the player who knows more gets it",
  "wrong. \"Need You Now\", \"Stupid Boy\", \"By The Way\" and \"That's Life\" all came out of `hers`",
  "for that reason, and two more came out because they are Ariana Grande tracks sitting on this",
  "app's own guest shelf. The test is RECOGNITION, not existence: many songs are called \"Tell",
  "Me\" and none of them owns it, so that one stays.",
  "",
  "THE TWO SIDES MUST NOT SHARE A TITLE, or near enough to one. \"Tennessee\" was on both lists",
  "(Sugarland, 2004) and \"Firefly\" sat one letter from Faith Hill's \"Fireflies\", which is still",
  "a decoy here. Both came out of `hers`. Nothing here collides with her released catalogue or",
  "with any guest shelf either — Lee Ann Womack's album alone offered \"The Last Time\" and",
  "\"Happiness\", and both were caught on the way in.",
  "",
  "`hers` CARRIES NO YEARS AND MUST NOT BE GIVEN ANY. Most of these were never released, so the",
  "dates either do not exist or are guesses, and a guessed year used as a bar would throw out",
  "good titles on bad evidence. The era bound is enforced by reading instead — see the roster",
  "entry in js/config.js. The decoys ARE dated, because theirs are real release years, and that",
  "is what a reveal can print.",
  "",
  "THE DEAL IS KEPT NEAR EVEN. At these counts neither \"always say hers\" nor \"always say not",
  "hers\" pays, which is the same fairness argument that set Aaron or Jack's four-record pool.",
  "Adding to one side without the other reopens it.",
  "",
  "ANYTHING ADDED LATER RUNS THE WHOLE SWEEP AGAIN: released catalogue, every guest shelf, the",
  "other pool, singles by discography, and a dominant-owner read."
 ],
